{"assets":["/editorial-assets/ea1df6c27767f6c3873ac2e78126d62d877ec6477ff8262d4a4e687346d14330.png","/editorial-assets/8fb886c2520cc4c3276f25cb1fea7b30eeb3c999c204abed7c7dc7df33560a91.png","/editorial-assets/523a586bdba35422f9fc2f3b7bf5eb117ded7bb47da95b216e93041aa2b7151c.png","/editorial-assets/18d5abe342a454bbbcf910d65070d6ca927ebbfd1d13fceee0590c03b55a33b3.png","/editorial-assets/e462b5fb7dc45c1a12e542513e3ee7348cb9dd13fe33c381cff2d409f6e646ee.png","/editorial-assets/e1e8998c9b274721619ef491ccb8d99b8d8e733f9f360a1b315d4bb4019d5f17.png","/editorial-assets/7a21bc3d1b41fdc5856643613b6c201e685b61749a7335c3f220d0ec61d8f4b9.png","/editorial-assets/4313f3afb25c1b815867efcd0adc15d014d66447d6a13b1af26c5ae79703ce4f.png","/editorial-assets/8312a25b5012d5390682b587ca881e715c4e46c09f3336c58a710b4aedc2a81a.png","/editorial-assets/dceea6d0f4543ecdcfe566aa220f09cd055a68b1de6b1ae41465a7efcaf9926f.png","/editorial-assets/f6b6006e513b887648a1afbc68df436da3cc3faf20cf999f3994f109f73d3999.png","/editorial-assets/474c9d73cf72f62bc463f2f328b71e0906bbf3c934d23397227c7d51da1b786a.png","/editorial-assets/f869bb5d7dba55bca637793946a1f27bc6abce91728a546547c6c9f2664d4d6b.png","/editorial-assets/651b31474b99cbca1149cab808e6030c2b42a9b7980e10a766124fe4f6bd0c52.png","/editorial-assets/d908ea2a38f02a86282228c345b0c241a5361d2c5e6ea1b93e61c7a53590927c.png","/editorial-assets/f4320d43f30e9833ddc48d2b45bb066e54334df50a8f9cd0b012615722650a63.png","/editorial-assets/4ad861fd19e9b26b0819d58ed8aec11667615a175f1bba7f4b7f3e44be1ea220.png","/editorial-assets/4f4dc4e1a67dabceae704fd67c97cf266036349d2984935e6aa093d0fa771dee.png","/editorial-assets/50fac32ba43fcba8c18e3e6d955376887a58b2b3e4a253457e0d304e74c5c878.png","/editorial-assets/fe2061c522c04f5e29fda64e0fa48953b897ca30197b0acfd9ed6b5dbe9ac809.png","/editorial-assets/d1b4dcacdafb52a56085988b7b698619bbca52fa9d0fff9848c5acb0a43e7b52.png","/editorial-assets/d35bd8abd44497b40c99eaa9d92ce1ac79cb71a5f37b49e1364193b6d14f750a.png","/editorial-assets/a886b3997a8a5f9b8547c4d0ac0d2b24f33b4d6e8e8ba14df6461b7ee1effd3b.png","/editorial-assets/f9edda04d142988747c93c4e7ffb9141d5687b7dd90520c29948f28573b3d1a1.png","/editorial-assets/3cece793258198f4862d9218991ab6604b90af03942d2031c198727eada0023c.png","/editorial-assets/bbd012a2a04f145045ba79bcdb66670ea821f8b45afec6c77b0bb54094d03f99.png","/editorial-assets/3e4fb4788c946a11f130447bf2b397ea820103b2303039b59e665b30cd2ac022.png","/editorial-assets/c91a0b5502c991cb46e8d72f03f1ad36c8a84d2d7f2dfb9e47db825e970c0024.png","/editorial-assets/93b99b37b67ca984aa7ad91786b2df707d9edb8a55fb079eb06c816ac076effa.png","/editorial-assets/b1b75b6186b573fc169cd010bfbd15b578a8c702c4f5daf67ae1f6a49224eeb1.png","/editorial-assets/74ab1bab73db126cd028c34b5b0258e6c417217ef1ec9adffb917dd4152692ac.png","/editorial-assets/2523d9bf3cc87c7d84252b36843d44cbb90248e979607e663343c3cf0ef8262a.png"],"contestId":"950","diagnostics":[],"document":{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Besides the editorial we prepared some challenges for you."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"950A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/950/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"950A - Left-handers, Right-handers and Ambidexters"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Iterate over size of the team. Now you know how many players should play with left hand, but are not left-handed (because there are no so much left-handed players). The same with right hand. Just check if sum of these values is not more than number of ambidexters."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (easy): Solve this problem in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"(1) time."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (easy): Now you have many millipedes with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" hands. Each millipede can either play with one hand or is ambidexter and can play with any hand. For each number of hand you know number of millipedes can play with it and number of ambidexters. Find out maximum number of team of millipedes in which there is equal amount of millipedes which play with some hand."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/MikeMirzayanov"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"MikeMirzayanov"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", developing — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/fcspartakm"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"fcspartakm"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"950B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/950/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"950B - Intercepted Message"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's define "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/ea1df6c27767f6c3873ac2e78126d62d877ec6477ff8262d4a4e687346d14330.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/8fb886c2520cc4c3276f25cb1fea7b30eeb3c999c204abed7c7dc7df33560a91.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" as sums of first "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/523a586bdba35422f9fc2f3b7bf5eb117ded7bb47da95b216e93041aa2b7151c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" elements of "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/18d5abe342a454bbbcf910d65070d6ca927ebbfd1d13fceee0590c03b55a33b3.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e462b5fb7dc45c1a12e542513e3ee7348cb9dd13fe33c381cff2d409f6e646ee.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" ("},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/e1e8998c9b274721619ef491ccb8d99b8d8e733f9f360a1b315d4bb4019d5f17.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/7a21bc3d1b41fdc5856643613b6c201e685b61749a7335c3f220d0ec61d8f4b9.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4313f3afb25c1b815867efcd0adc15d014d66447d6a13b1af26c5ae79703ce4f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/8312a25b5012d5390682b587ca881e715c4e46c09f3336c58a710b4aedc2a81a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"). "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/dceea6d0f4543ecdcfe566aa220f09cd055a68b1de6b1ae41465a7efcaf9926f.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f6b6006e513b887648a1afbc68df436da3cc3faf20cf999f3994f109f73d3999.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" can be same file iff this three conditions are true:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":true},"children":[{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/474c9d73cf72f62bc463f2f328b71e0906bbf3c934d23397227c7d51da1b786a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" because we need to divide prefix into files. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f869bb5d7dba55bca637793946a1f27bc6abce91728a546547c6c9f2664d4d6b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" because we need to divide suffix into files. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/651b31474b99cbca1149cab808e6030c2b42a9b7980e10a766124fe4f6bd0c52.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" segments have same sum. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It's easy to see that if two first conditions are true then the third are true too because "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d908ea2a38f02a86282228c345b0c241a5361d2c5e6ea1b93e61c7a53590927c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" and because of this fact and condition from statement "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f4320d43f30e9833ddc48d2b45bb066e54334df50a8f9cd0b012615722650a63.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" answer is a number of non-empty prefixes with the same sum."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Time complexity is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4ad861fd19e9b26b0819d58ed8aec11667615a175f1bba7f4b7f3e44be1ea220.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" if you use two pointers or "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/4f4dc4e1a67dabceae704fd67c97cf266036349d2984935e6aa093d0fa771dee.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" if you use some data structure."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (easy): You lost one block from second message and you interested in maximal possible number of files if you can insert this block anywhere in second message. In this case it graduated that "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/50fac32ba43fcba8c18e3e6d955376887a58b2b3e4a253457e0d304e74c5c878.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/meshanya"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"meshanya"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", developing — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Zlobober"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Zlobober"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/KAN"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"KAN"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"949A"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/949/problem/A"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"949A - Zebras"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Simple greedy works here. Let's go from left to right and assign each element to some subsequence. At each moment we have two types of already built subsequences: zebras (\"0\", \"010\", \"01010\", ...) and \"almost zebras\" (\"01\", \"0101\", \"010101\"). If next element of the string is '1' we should add it to some zebra making it \"almost zebra\". If there are no zebras at this moment it's impossible to divide string into zebra subsequences. If next element of the string is '0' we should add it so some \"almost zebra\" making it simple zebra. If there are no \"almost zebra\"'s now just create new zebra consisting of this '0'."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"If there are no \"almost zebra\"'s at the end answer exists and built zebras satisfy all requirements otherwise there is no answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (easy): Find answer minimizing length of longest subsequence."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (hard): Find answer minimizing number of subsequences."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Zlobober"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Zlobober"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", developing — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/ch_egor"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ch_egor"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"949B"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/949/problem/B"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"949B - A Leapfrog in the Array"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In odd position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" value "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/fe2061c522c04f5e29fda64e0fa48953b897ca30197b0acfd9ed6b5dbe9ac809.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" will be set. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"For even position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" let's find out position from which value has arrived and iterate over such position until we will arrive to odd position for which we know answer."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"At the moment of jumping to cell "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" there are "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d1b4dcacdafb52a56085988b7b698619bbca52fa9d0fff9848c5acb0a43e7b52.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" elements to the right of the position "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". So there are "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/d35bd8abd44497b40c99eaa9d92ce1ac79cb71a5f37b49e1364193b6d14f750a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" elements to the right of this position and jump to cell "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"p"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" was done from position "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/a886b3997a8a5f9b8547c4d0ac0d2b24f33b4d6e8e8ba14df6461b7ee1effd3b.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". During each such jump length of jump decreases at least by 2 times, so there are no more than "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/f9edda04d142988747c93c4e7ffb9141d5687b7dd90520c29948f28573b3d1a1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" jumps and solution works in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3cece793258198f4862d9218991ab6604b90af03942d2031c198727eada0023c.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea and developng — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Sender"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Sender"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"949C"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/949/problem/C"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"949C - Data Center Maintenance"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Formally you are given a properly colored graph and you are asked to find out size of smallest non-empty subset such that after addition 1 modulo "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"h"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to colors of vertices in this subset coloring will remain proper."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's build a directed graph with "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" vertices and edge from "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" iff "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"u"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"v"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" are connected by edge in original graph and "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/bbd012a2a04f145045ba79bcdb66670ea821f8b45afec6c77b0bb54094d03f99.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":". Now let's fix some vertex which color will be changed. It's clear that we should take into its set all vertices which are reachable from it. Now our problem is reduced to following problem: \"Given directed graph find vertex with smallest number reachable from it vertices\". It's just any vertex from smallest strongly connected component which is sink (strongly connected component such there is no strongly connected component reachable from it)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (medium): Given graph is not properly colored, but you don't have to minimize size of the set. Can you solve it?"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS ((NP?)-hard): Given graph is not properly colored, but you have to minimize size of the set."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/V--o_o--V"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"V--o_o--V"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", developer — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Flyrise"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Flyrise"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"949D"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/949/problem/D"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"949D - Curfew"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's solve the problem in case when there is only one instructor (which moves from left to right and the only goal is to minimize number of bad rooms)"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I claim, that the following greedy works: "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Move through rooms from left to right "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" If there are too many students inside room, send the excess students to the next room "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" If there are not enough students, but it is possible to fulfill this room from rooms on the right (the sum "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/3e4fb4788c946a11f130447bf2b397ea820103b2303039b59e665b30cd2ac022.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" is at least "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), then do it. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" If it's not possible, then send all students to the following room. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" If it is the last room, say that those students are hiding in it. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This greedy can be implemented in "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/c91a0b5502c991cb46e8d72f03f1ad36c8a84d2d7f2dfb9e47db825e970c0024.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" time: calculate the prefix sums on the initial "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", this way you can check if it is possible to move students from following rooms here fast."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"To handle the removal students from following rooms you can maintain the current \"debt\" of students. When you first consider room you can repay the debt as much as you can and then check one of the cases above. Since the both left and right borders of segments are moving monotonously the debt will be \"inherited\" correctly."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Notice, that you can only consider \"paths of different students never cross\", that means if first student was initially in room "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and moved to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", while the second student was in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and moved to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", then if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"j"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". Because otherwise you can swap students and nothing will change."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"The proof of the greedy (you can possibly skip it)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Suppose there is a better answer, which completes the rooms "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", while the greedy solutions completes rooms "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"1"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", ..., "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"l"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"k"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We will assume that in optimal solution paths of students don't intersect, that all \"excessive\" students are hiding in last room and that all rooms in optimal answer are either full ("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") or empty (0). Otherwise it's possible to change the \"correct answer in such way, that number of good rooms will not decrease."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is smallest index when "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≠ "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":". Then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", because greedy solution would always fulfill the room "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" if it would be possible (actually, greedy solution builts the lexmin solution)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"But if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" > "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" we can \"patch\" the supposed optimal solution and move all students which were sent to room "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" (we know it is possible by the greedy solution's answer)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"This way we can increase the common prefix with any possible \"best\" answer hence contradiction."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Back to the problem with two instructors. Recall, that \"paths of different students don't cross\", hence there exists a \"border\", the number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" from 0 to "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"nb"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":", where the first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" students are going to the first instructor and all others to second."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"One could have bruteforced that border and solved the both halfs of the array by the method above, but then the complexity will be "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"·"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"b"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" which is too much. We need to search for the border more efficiently."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") will be the answer for first instructor, when he is given "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" first students and "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is the answer for second instructor when he is given all students except first "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ones."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"It is easy to see, that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") in decreasing, while "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is increasing (both times it is not strict monotonicity). Indeed, the more students are given to instructor, than more opportunities he has (all excessive students can always hide, so it is not a problem)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"We are searching for "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ans"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"max"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"), "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":")) is smallest possible.Let's introduce function "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") = "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"f"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") — increasing (but still not strict)."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's call "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" the smallest index, such that "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"z"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") ≥ 0. One can see, that a "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"min"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ans"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1), "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ans"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")) is the final answer. Indeed, if one will try greater "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"'s than "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", than the "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"g"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") will be dominating in max, and hence "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ans"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":") is more optimal. Otherwise, if "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" < "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"0"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" - 1, then "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"ans"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"m"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") is better."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Bonus (medium): Solve this problem in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":") time."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Bonus (medium): Students became fat and can't hide under the bed! Can you solve it now?"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Sender"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Sender"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", developer — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/cdkrot"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"cdkrot"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"949E"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/949/problem/E"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"949E - Binary Cards"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"There are two observations required to solve this problem:"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" You don't have to take two cards with same number. If you took two cards with number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" you can take card with number 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and card with number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and answer will remain correct. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" If you took card with number "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" you don't have to take card with number  - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":". You can take cards 2"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" and  - "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"x"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" instead. "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Consider all numbers. If there are no odd numbers you don't have to take 1 or  - 1 cards. Otherwise you have to take either 1 or  - 1. Try both possibilities and add value of taken card to all odd numbers. After this step all numbers are even, so you can just divide them by 2 and solve the same problem with divided number. After each step of this algorithm maximum possible absolute value of card is also divided by 2, so in worst case complexity will be "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/93b99b37b67ca984aa7ad91786b2df707d9edb8a55fb079eb06c816ac076effa.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":", where "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" is a maximum absolute value of number. Solution of the equation is "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/b1b75b6186b573fc169cd010bfbd15b578a8c702c4f5daf67ae1f6a49224eeb1.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":" so it's fast enough. "}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (medium-hard): "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 1000, |"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"| ≤ 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"18"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (hard): "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":" ≤ 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"5"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":", |"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"a"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"i"}],"kind":"emphasis"}],"kind":"subscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"| ≤ 10"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"18"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":"."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS: What about ternary cards?"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/Zlobober"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Zlobober"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":", developer — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/malcolm"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"malcolm"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"problemCode":"949F"},"children":[{"attrs":{"level":3},"children":[{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/contest/949/problem/F"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"949F - Astronomy"}],"kind":"link"}],"kind":"heading"},{"children":[{"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Let's shuffle lines randomly. Select some 4 points and find their intersection point. If it's not integer or is not in bounding box it's definitely not an answer. Otherwise let's check it. Iterate over points and check if there is another point on the line that going through answer and this point. To check it we need to build some structure that can check if some line is inside it. For example you can for each point sort other points by polar angle, or store polar angles in hash table (multiplying it by number of order "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"C"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":" making different angles different). If there is no pair for some point let's stop checking it. This solution works in "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"3"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"In fact no, it's "},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"O"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"kind":"text","text":"("},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"n"}],"kind":"emphasis"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"2"}],"kind":"superscript"},{"kind":"text","text":")."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"I will write full formal proof soon, now I'll write just sketch of it."}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"attrs":{"ordered":false},"children":[{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Consider planar graph where vertices are all intersection points of some lines passing through two given points. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Let's find expected number of lines checking. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Divide vertices into 2 classes: heavy (with degree "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/74ab1bab73db126cd028c34b5b0258e6c417217ef1ec9adffb917dd4152692ac.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":") and light (with degree "},{"attrs":{"alt":"","src":"/editorial-assets/2523d9bf3cc87c7d84252b36843d44cbb90248e979607e663343c3cf0ef8262a.png"},"kind":"image"},{"kind":"text","text":") "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" Expected time of checking is sum of expected time checking light vertices and heavy vertices "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" It's quite easy to prove it for light vertices. "}],"kind":"list_item"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":" To proof it for heavy vertices "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"Szemerédi–Trotter"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":" theorem might be useful "}],"kind":"list_item"}],"kind":"list"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS (medium): It was a challenge to prepare tests for this problem. Which tests requires many points/lines checking?"}],"kind":"paragraph"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"BONUS: There are many solutions that works in this problem. I will be glad to see your approaches (correct or not, proved or not) in comments."}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"container"}],"kind":"problem_section"},{"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"(Idea and developng — "},{"attrs":{"href":"https://codeforces.com/profile/vintage_Vlad_Makeev"},"children":[{"kind":"text","text":"vintage_Vlad_Makeev"}],"kind":"link"},{"kind":"text","text":")"}],"kind":"paragraph"}],"kind":"document"},"schema":2,"sourceUrl":"https://codeforces.com/blog/entry/58291"}